Changed from upstream (each change is marked "(Not in upstream.)" in the file):
  src/colormap.rs   two more passes of Lloyd's algorithm over the histogram after upstream's two (up to 24 passes, until the error stops falling, for
      a histogram of 1024 entries or fewer), cut into four ranges that run on threads for 8192 entries or more; the sums are added in range order, so
      the palette does not depend on the number of threads. A histogram of more than 1,048,576 entries keeps upstream's two passes.
  src/cluster.rs, src/options.rs, src/quantize.rs   a target error: Options::set_target_mse lets the palette have fewer colours than asked for while the
      mean squared error of a colour channel stays at or below it (the median cut stops splitting, Ward's agglomeration goes on merging).
  src/cluster.rs, src/quantize.rs   a histogram of more colours than asked for and at most 1024 entries is reduced by Ward's agglomeration of
      its entries (merge the pair that adds the least squared error) where upstream splits it by median cut.
  src/quantize.rs   the dithering loop runs in four horizontal bands on up to four threads for a picture of 100,000 pixels or more; the error that
      would pass from a band's last row to the next band's first row is dropped, so the indices differ slightly from upstream's and do not depend
      on the number of threads.
  src/quantize.rs, src/colormap.rs   a cache of the nearest palette entry by exact pixel colour in the two remap loops, and no error passed on by a
      pixel that the palette holds exactly. A flat area, or a picture of at most 256 colours, is looked up instead of searched (same output).

Followed, not copied:
  example/src/main.rs              -> src/main.rs    the calls of the library (Options, set_max_colors, QuantizeResult::quantize,
      set_dithering_level, remap_image, get_palette) are the example's, in its order. Everything else is ours: the input (the width and the
      height as two little-endian u32, then RGBA bytes, on standard input, bounded by MAX_PIXELS), the output (the palette and the indices
      on standard output) and the argument parser. The example reads and writes PNG files through the png crate, which this program does not use.

The library is built from ./quantizr (a path dependency), not from crates.io, so that the project can change it in measured steps (the palette
size, more k-means passes, the dither). The program has no other dependency. The program is built by scripts/install-quantizr-pixels.sh with the
pinned Rust toolchain, for the musl target.
Additions of ours: [profile.release] panic = "abort", lto = "thin" and codegen-units = 1 in Cargo.toml (3 to 4 percent of the run on a 768 x 512
picture, linux/arm64; the palette and the indices are the same bytes).

Cargo.lock                         ours: written by cargo for the two crates; quantizr is a path dependency, so the lockfile holds no checksum.
